When testing a conjecture  one sometimes wants to run a large number of
randomly chosen examples. Here's some typical code that one might use to study
a random graph ideal.  First we use ``for ... list ...'' to construct a list L
and suppress its printing by ending the line that creates it with a ``;''.
Each entry of L is a triple consisting of the codimension, degree, and Betti
table of a random graph ideal on 10 vertices having only 4 edges.

+--------------------------------------------+
|i93 : R = ZZ/2[vars(0..10)]                 |
|                                            |
|o93 = R                                     |
|                                            |
|o93 : PolynomialRing                        |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i94 : L=for j from 1 to 100 list(           |
|          I = edgeIdeal randomGraph (R,5);  |
|          (codim I, degree I, betti res I));|
+--------------------------------------------+

We can use *note tally: (Macaulay2Doc)tally, to find out how many examples were
found with each combination of codimension and degree and Betti table.

